@@s001 BFS: breadth-first visit
@@s002 ora:node just extracted,dato:in the queue,ok:visited, with its distance
@@s003 Source <b>s</b>. I put s in the queue and mark <code>d(s) = 0</code>. The queue is FIFO: whoever entered first leaves first.
@@s004 I extract <b>s</b>. The unseen neighbours are <b>a</b> and <b>b</b>: they get <code>d = d(s) + 1 = 1</code> and parent s. I put them in the queue.
@@s005 I extract <b>a</b>. The neighbour <b>s</b> is already seen: I skip it. The neighbour <b>c</b> is new: <code>d(c) = 2</code>.
@@s006 I extract <b>b</b>. Neighbours: s (seen), <b>d</b> (new, <code>d = 2</code>).
@@s007 I extract <b>c</b>. Neighbours: a (seen), <b>e</b> (new, <code>d = 3</code>).
@@s008 I extract <b>d</b>. Its neighbour e is already in the queue with <code>d = 3</code>: the distance from d (2 + 1) is the same, nothing changes.
@@s009 I extract <b>e</b>: no new neighbours. The queue is empty: the BFS is finished. Distances: s 0, a and b 1, c and d 2, e 3.
@@s010 dato:layer 0,ora:layer 1,no:arc inside a layer (odd cycle)
@@s011 I do a BFS from <b>a</b>. The layers are: layer 0 = {a}, layer 1 = the neighbours of a.
@@s012 Layer 1: <b>b, c, d</b>. Colour them with two alternating colours: the rule is that every arc connects two <b>adjacent</b> layers.
@@s013 Now the arc <b>b–c</b>: it connects two nodes <b>of the same layer</b>. If I colour them differently, the colour does not come back: there is a cycle of odd length (a, b, c).
@@s014 BFS: breadth-first visit
@@s015 The BFS (breadth-first search, visit in breadth) explores the graph by layers: first all the nodes at distance 1, then at distance 2, and so on. It is the right algorithm every time the question is "how many steps at minimum".
@@s016 The problem and the idea
@@s017 The problem: from a source <code>s</code> I want to know which nodes I can reach and, for each, the <b>minimum number of arcs</b> (the <b>distance</b>, <code>d(v)</code>). The graph has no weights: each arc is worth 1.
@@s018 The idea is a <b>FIFO queue</b> (first in, first out). The node that entered first leaves first. So the nodes come out in order of distance: first those at distance 0, then 1, then 2. It is the same as going down by layers from the source.
@@s019 BFS from s: the queue and the distances
@@s020 The steps, one by one
@@s021 Put <code>s</code> in the queue, <code>d(s) = 0</code>, mark <code>s</code> as seen.
@@s022 Extract the first node <code>u</code> from the queue.
@@s023 For each neighbour <code>v</code> not yet seen: <code>d(v) = d(u) + 1</code>, <code>parent(v) = u</code>, mark <code>v</code> and put it in the queue.
@@s024 Repeat from step 2 until the queue is empty.
@@s025 The <b>parent</b> of a node is the node from which you discovered it. Following the parents backwards you rebuild the minimum path.
@@s026 Seen at the right moment
@@s027 A node is marked as seen <b>when it enters the queue</b>, not when it leaves. If you mark it on exit, it can enter the queue several times and the BFS becomes slow, even though the result remains correct.
@@s028 Why it works
@@s029 The queue makes the nodes come out in non-decreasing order of distance. So the <b>first time</b> you meet a node <code>v</code> you arrive at it by the shortest possible path. Every arc of the undirected graph connects two nodes of layers that differ by at most 1: that is why no shorter path can escape the layers.
@@s030 The reason in one sentence
@@s031 The queue is always sorted by distance, and a node more distant than one already in the queue plus one step is never added.
@@s032 Cost and when to use it
@@s033 Every node enters the queue once only, and every arc is looked at at most twice. Cost: <code>O(n + m)</code>, linear in the size of the graph.
@@s034 Use the BFS when the question is one of these:
@@s035 <b>reachability</b>: "from s can I reach t?", "which nodes can I reach?";
@@s036 <b>minimum distance without weights</b>: "the minimum number of moves", "the minimum number of passages";
@@s037 <b>connected components</b>: a BFS for each node not yet visited;
@@s038 <b>bipartiteness</b>: see the following section.
@@s039 With weights different from 1 the BFS is wrong: use Dijkstra (weights ≥ 0) or Bellman-Ford (weights also negative).
@@s040 The bipartiteness test
@@s041 A graph is <b>bipartite</b> if its nodes can be coloured with two colours without two adjacent nodes having the same colour. Equivalently: it has no cycles of odd length.
@@s042 To check it, do a BFS and colour each layer with a different colour. If an arc connects two nodes of the <b>same layer</b>, you have found an odd cycle and the graph is not bipartite.
@@s043 An arc inside a layer reveals an odd cycle
@@s044 The code, side by side
@@s045 This page explains the concept. The Java code that implements it, with a line-by-line explanation and the syntax notes needed, is on the side-by-side page.
